      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "Checked by loading `tool_utils.py` from `main` and from this head and calling the default `tool_formatter` on both (I could not run the pytest module here because the test package imports `trl`).\n\nString enums are byte-identical to `main` for `[\"small\", \"large\"]`, `[\"a, b\", \"c\"]`, `[\"默认\", \"x\"]`, `[\"1\", \"2\"]` and the empty list, both flat and OpenAI-wrapped, so existing prompts do not change. For non-string enums `main` raises `TypeError: sequence item 0: expected str instance` on `[1, 2, 3]`, `[0.5, 1.5]`, `[True, False]`, `[None]` and on dict or list members; this head renders `1, 2, 3`, `0.5, 1.5`, `true, false`, `null` and `{\"a\": 1}, [1, 2]`. Flat and wrapped tools render the same. The `enum` join in `tool_formatter` is the only place in this file that formats enum values, so no sibling site needs the same change.\n\nNothing blocking. One optional observation: a mixed enum such as `[\"1\", 1]` renders as `[1, 1]`, so the prompt cannot tell the string \"1\" from the integer 1. Leaving strings unquoted is what keeps existing prompts unchanged, so I would keep it; a sentence in the docstring or the PR description would be enough.\n",
